WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.920
Bump and Jump, the Sunday drive that turned into a nightmare on wheels.

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:28.000
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:29.080
I'm Mike.

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:31.280
I'm Sean. And I'm Sam.

00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:35.600
Guys, today we have Bump and Jump.

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:40.860
Didn't know that the game took place on a Sunday, but it is definitely a nightmare on wheels.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:48.020
It's quite the interesting storyline for this one in the manual.

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:54.020
If you didn't know any better, Bump and Jump is actually like the cocaine cliff diving game,

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:56.860
where you just take a bump and then jump off a cliff.

00:00:57.840 --> 00:00:59.840
Oh, wow. I never actually thought about that.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.080
That's the 80s, man.

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:04.220
Yeah, there you go. That's a lot of people were doing that in the 80s.

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:06.260
It's on someone's best of 88 list.

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:07.640
Right, right.

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:14.600
Of course, Bump and Jump, you know, as a game, is a Data East game.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:17.500
But I'm curious, like, which one hit first?

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:22.760
The realization that it was a Data East game or the realization that it was a driving game?

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:30.600
Because Bump and Jump doesn't quite scream to me that you're going to be like doing a top-down arcade racing style game.

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:37.060
Well, it hit me because I always Google it first before I boot anything up.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:44.280
And, you know, it's just that Wikipedia entry that says, like, Bump and Jump is a 1988 Data East.

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:46.140
And then I just sort of lose it.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:48.820
I'm just like, my eyes glaze over a little bit.

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:56.660
Then I clicked on it and I saw the gameplay section and I'm like, okay, this sounds like they can't mess it up too bad.

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:00.740
That was my little thing there.

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:11.900
And this is one of those rare ones too where it's like, it's an arcade game, pretty popular, that is now finally coming to the NES after being on the Famicom, like, years before.

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:21.900
And I say rare only because we cover every NES game, but some people might just only own these, like, arcade games that then get ported to the NES.

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:27.520
Sam, what are you feeling about, like, these inferior NES ports of these arcade games?

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:40.160
I kind of have mixed feelings about them because on the one hand, I imagine that the idea of having an arcade game in your house must have been really novel in the 80s and must have been pretty cool.

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:43.840
And you kind of understand that it's not going to be the same experience as going to the arcade.

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:46.540
But on the other hand, most of the time they suck.

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:48.240
So I don't know.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:56.260
Yeah, I actually wonder, if you think about it, right, an arcade game was probably 25 cents, right?

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:57.540
Yeah. Per play.

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:01.920
That means that it would take 240 quarters to get to $60.

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:13.840
Do you think that people would play bump and jump 240 times at the arcade before they, like, reach the same limit of like, well, I'm glad I bought it for home play instead.

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:16.860
So this way I didn't spend any additional quarters.

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:22.940
So you're actually asking, would you want to play this game 240 times?

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:24.940
Yeah, yeah. Okay.

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:26.140
Or any arcade game.

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:28.120
Yeah. 240 times.

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:30.200
I don't know. How often do people go to the arcade?

00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:31.820
It's like a Saturday thing, right?

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:32.760
You would just hang out there.

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:35.100
From the movies, it seems like it was pretty often.

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:36.160
They're always there.

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:43.020
Yeah. Well, if you go every Saturday, let's say for a year, that's 52 times if you only play a game once.

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:49.040
So I imagine that if someone is really into arcades, they would probably be playing some of these games a couple hundred times.

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:51.280
And there's definitely like the greats.

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:57.420
I'm sure people would say that Donkey Kong, Pac-Man, Millipede.

00:03:57.620 --> 00:04:00.060
Well, that's like at least a five quarter game right there.

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:03.960
Yeah, exactly. Like you would put a lot of quarters into those games.

00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:18.220
You just can't realistically be putting like, you know, for every arcade game that got ported to the NES, that number times 240 quarters and being like, yeah, I spent like 1300 quarters at the arcade last year.

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:26.380
What if it's like you get that as a present, you get the NES game as a present or something because your mom or someone knows that you love the arcade game so much.

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:31.640
And then you use the NES one to practice on because it's a lot harder and the controls are a lot more clunky.

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:36.480
And then when you get to the actual one, maybe it helps, but maybe it doesn't.

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:39.880
I don't know. I think that goes back to the novelty part that you mentioned, Sam.

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:49.320
And I think that is the big selling point of like, wow, this huge machine with a CRT built into it that I go to like, I go out to the mall and I play it there.

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:53.400
Now I finally like just have it in my living room and I can invite my friends over.

00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:54.720
Now I'm the arcade.

00:04:56.660 --> 00:05:06.300
Yeah, I mean, I don't, I don't really get that, uh, that logic in that like home gaming is practice and the arcade is the actual thing.

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:14.860
Like, I mean, I guess like, yeah, the arcade version was definitely a better version, but it's not like, uh, you're playing competitively.

00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:16.240
I don't know. Funny.

00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:25.260
And to be fair, you don't have to spend 240 quarters to be like, well, I got the same value as those fools that bought it on the, on the, on the home system.

00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:27.860
Social interactions. Right, right.

00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:31.460
Uh, I just think it's like a funny argument to actually like say that number out loud.

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:42.440
I bet nobody has thought that like you have to spend 240 quarters to equal the same price, but you could actually just like rent it in your, in your mind in a way by going to the arcade and just spending less.

00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:49.320
These are the threshold numbers we think about, you know, that's the Fibonacci sequence of home video gaming.

00:05:49.580 --> 00:05:54.940
So do you think that someone would have, would play the game less in the arcade if they had it at home?

00:05:54.980 --> 00:05:59.120
Or do you think they would play it more because it's like a favorite of theirs?

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:00.740
Ask yourself that same question.

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:02.260
What would you do? Give me a game.

00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:04.780
Like, all right. So just Donkey Kong, right?

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:06.720
That's on the NES, but it's also in the arcade.

00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:09.380
Are you going to play that more or less?

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:11.020
I'm not saying, would you play it at the arcade?

00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:14.140
I'm saying you're going to play it more or less now that you own it at home.

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:17.260
Probably less. Yeah.

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:18.800
I would agree with less.

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:23.400
For that game. But I think it also depends on the kind of games where the port isn't as good.

00:06:23.780 --> 00:06:25.700
Yeah. Like what was it?

00:06:25.820 --> 00:06:26.920
Ikari Warriors or something?

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:29.120
Yeah. That was a bad part.

00:06:29.800 --> 00:06:30.960
You take that one.

00:06:31.160 --> 00:06:34.920
Like, are you going to play it more in the arcade because you've kind of been teased by

00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:37.160
the crappy version of it at home?

00:06:37.160 --> 00:06:39.060
And maybe you don't think that that's crappy.

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:42.800
Maybe you don't even know yet what a real Ikari Warriors can be like.

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:44.240
So you're like, oh, I have that at home.

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:47.660
And then you like see all the flashes on the screen and you go over and you're like, what

00:06:47.660 --> 00:06:48.540
are those graphics?

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:50.120
Like, let me get in there.

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:54.260
And then you realize like playing with, uh, what do we call, I guess we call those joysticks.

00:06:54.900 --> 00:07:00.000
Um, playing with those instead of a D-pad is also really effective for a game like Ikari

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:04.200
Warriors where you can choose where you shoot in which direction and stuff like that.

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:08.640
So do we know what the experience of bump and jump was like in the arcade?

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:11.120
Well, I will tell you this.

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:14.780
Uh, it was a less complex game in the arcade.

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:21.080
Oh, interesting. Yeah. Uh, the NES version of the game is the one that requires players to pick up cans of gasoline

00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:23.600
to, uh, continue to collect fuel.

00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:27.220
That was just not an element in the arcade version of the game.

00:07:27.620 --> 00:07:30.440
So, you know, I actually think that's shocking.

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:35.660
Like you, the picking up gas thing seems like totally an arcade mechanic of like, let's make

00:07:35.660 --> 00:07:39.960
sure we screw over the player in every corner so that they keep putting in corner quarters.

00:07:40.280 --> 00:07:47.860
I mean, maybe, maybe the gas was only put in to, uh, emulate the obstacles, uh, that

00:07:47.860 --> 00:07:49.180
quarters would have been.

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:50.560
Uh, right, right.

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:53.660
To make it feel like you're getting that arcade experience at home.

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:56.720
Yeah. To artificially extend the game.

00:07:57.140 --> 00:08:02.980
Yeah. There should have been an adapter on the NES to just like insert a 25 cent slot, you know,

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:04.840
onto the back of your NES or something.

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:06.680
Can we talk about those cans for a second?

00:08:06.820 --> 00:08:08.200
The gas cans? Yeah.

00:08:08.360 --> 00:08:09.320
Talk about the gas cans.

00:08:09.560 --> 00:08:15.920
They, I feel like whoever coded this to put the gas cans in just kind of said, all right,

00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:19.360
as long as it's on the screen somewhere, then that's, that's fine.

00:08:19.540 --> 00:08:25.080
Yeah. Because they're always in just, they're in the, the window dressing all the time and you

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:26.200
can't touch them. Yeah.

00:08:26.200 --> 00:08:31.320
They'd show up in the, like the boundaries of the course that show up in obstacles of the

00:08:31.320 --> 00:08:35.080
course that I've had one in the middle of the ocean as I was jumping over it.

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:39.160
So that's gotta be, I mean, Sam, you design games a little bit.

00:08:39.320 --> 00:08:45.180
That's gotta be just a way to say that like, this is all just randomly generated, right?

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:51.040
That they didn't design the actual course layouts in a way that like you would place the can,

00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:53.160
the gas cans meaningly, meaningfully.

00:08:53.540 --> 00:09:00.240
And so instead it's just like when they hit certain amount of part into the level or when

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:04.360
they're at this level of gas, then have a gas can appear.

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:09.120
And that just happens to be anywhere possible on the screen with whether it doesn't care whether

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:10.900
it's collectible for the player or not.

00:09:11.020 --> 00:09:16.260
Well, it does seem kind of random, but I don't know if it's like they show up in the same spot

00:09:16.260 --> 00:09:20.260
every time because I didn't really play the same level multiple times a lot.

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:27.260
Yeah. I'd just be shocked if they did though, show up in, um, in those places purposefully,

00:09:27.260 --> 00:09:32.120
because then that's just kind of like teasing the player of like, oh, you know, you could,

00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:34.240
you could get this gas can, but it'll kill you.

00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:37.320
And so it's not worth collecting, but you need those gas cans.

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:42.920
So you could be like, you could have four, um, you know, what are we going to call the

00:09:42.920 --> 00:09:43.940
unit of measurement here?

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:45.820
You could have four gas left.

00:09:46.340 --> 00:09:49.740
And every time you pick up a gas can, yeah, four power left.

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:52.880
And every time you pick up one of those gas cans, it gives you 10 more power.

00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:57.880
But if they place it in a place where you can't collect it, you are now dead no matter what.

00:09:58.260 --> 00:10:00.240
I would guess it's random because of that.

00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:04.260
And because I also see like cars in random spots too, like parked cars.

00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:06.600
Did you guys notice that? Yeah, I noticed that.

00:10:06.820 --> 00:10:10.880
There were like cars in the water or on the sides or in front of buildings and things.

00:10:10.880 --> 00:10:12.860
It's, it's, it's a, it's kind of inscrutable.

00:10:13.120 --> 00:10:20.560
Like I really couldn't tell if that was supposed to mean something or if it was, uh, just a, a quirk of the code.

00:10:20.940 --> 00:10:24.980
Um, but I mean, it seems like a lot of it was randomly generated.

00:10:25.140 --> 00:10:32.540
I don't know how much the, um, I don't know how much the levels themselves are because I mean, I can see chunks.

00:10:32.540 --> 00:10:38.820
Like I can see recognizable patterns in these levels, uh, or just like the road courses.

00:10:39.180 --> 00:10:43.340
But, uh, yeah, I can't, I can't tell just by deduction.

00:10:43.340 --> 00:10:44.320
Thank you.

00:11:27.700 --> 00:11:32.360
Yeah, well, you know, just to give the listeners a clearer picture here,

00:11:32.820 --> 00:11:37.340
Bump and Jump is an overhead view survival racing game.

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:40.320
I'm going to just describe it as a survival racing game

00:11:40.320 --> 00:11:43.500
because there's no discernible finish.

00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:45.780
You're just trying to survive every single obstacle

00:11:45.780 --> 00:11:49.060
until you get to the finish line of that stage,

00:11:49.060 --> 00:11:53.320
but it's one of those games where you just continue to progress forever, right?

00:11:53.320 --> 00:11:55.480
I don't think there is a clear ending.

00:11:55.640 --> 00:11:58.380
I think you can just keep going on to get a high score.

00:11:58.940 --> 00:12:02.680
Well, I mean, there are endings for each level.

00:12:03.020 --> 00:12:06.720
Like, you go to cross a finish line, it's easily recognizable,

00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:11.860
and then you start... Yeah, no, I'm just saying there's no final boss or anything,

00:12:12.020 --> 00:12:13.780
so you just keep going up in stages.

00:12:13.960 --> 00:12:16.020
It'd be like completing Pac-Man stages, you know?

00:12:16.160 --> 00:12:18.240
This is the closest thing to...

00:12:18.240 --> 00:12:23.300
This is just Spy Hunter again with a couple different mechanics,

00:12:23.860 --> 00:12:26.020
but I think Spy Hunter never stopped.

00:12:26.340 --> 00:12:27.700
Did Spy Hunter ever stop?

00:12:28.020 --> 00:12:28.920
That's a good question.

00:12:29.060 --> 00:12:30.240
I don't know. Yeah.

00:12:30.880 --> 00:12:36.380
So, yeah, it's a little bit more level-oriented than Spy Hunter, at least.

00:12:36.580 --> 00:12:39.900
Yeah, but did you like that breakup?

00:12:40.440 --> 00:12:42.160
You know, like, you seem to be a fan of it.

00:12:42.260 --> 00:12:44.580
You're saying at least it had that over Spy Hunter.

00:12:45.240 --> 00:12:48.420
I guess I don't know if I could qualitatively say

00:12:48.420 --> 00:12:49.860
that one was better than the other,

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:55.080
but I guess it does lend itself to, like,

00:12:55.200 --> 00:12:57.340
at least telling you you've gotten this far.

00:12:57.820 --> 00:12:59.600
So far, that's as much as I can say.

00:12:59.980 --> 00:13:02.540
I was going to say, I like how there's some courses,

00:13:02.800 --> 00:13:06.160
like, there's one that has a couple levels later,

00:13:06.280 --> 00:13:08.160
it's like five or six or something,

00:13:08.740 --> 00:13:13.560
that has these almost, like, rings around buildings,

00:13:13.560 --> 00:13:15.240
and the buildings are in the center of the map,

00:13:15.300 --> 00:13:16.900
and you have to jump to the side of them,

00:13:17.020 --> 00:13:18.380
and so I thought that was kind of cool.

00:13:18.740 --> 00:13:21.100
And then there was one or two after that,

00:13:21.220 --> 00:13:24.300
there was a level that had these sort of safe patches

00:13:24.300 --> 00:13:26.960
on the right side that were just roads that would continue,

00:13:27.340 --> 00:13:29.720
and no cars would ever spawn over there,

00:13:29.860 --> 00:13:32.060
so as long as you knew to jump over there,

00:13:32.400 --> 00:13:33.500
then you were safe.

00:13:33.640 --> 00:13:36.860
So I think that there are specifics about the levels

00:13:36.860 --> 00:13:38.740
that are different from each other

00:13:38.740 --> 00:13:42.580
that makes it, if not more enjoyable,

00:13:42.900 --> 00:13:46.860
then at least different and, like, kind of interesting.

00:13:47.320 --> 00:13:49.300
Yeah, I think they were trying to go for, like,

00:13:49.400 --> 00:13:52.620
a change of seasons effect between the levels

00:13:52.620 --> 00:13:53.600
and the color palettes.

00:13:53.820 --> 00:13:57.660
Yeah, I can see that. I'm sure it's more noticeable in the arcade version of the game,

00:13:57.680 --> 00:14:01.880
but even just the fact that the colors do change on the background tiles.

00:14:01.880 --> 00:14:05.780
There's a change in seasons and a change in urbanization.

00:14:06.460 --> 00:14:10.000
Yeah, I think that helps, like, break up the monotony of the game,

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:12.240
and it would be more jarring if just, like,

00:14:13.240 --> 00:14:15.220
it changed as you were driving.

00:14:15.420 --> 00:14:18.060
Like, it's like, oh, now you drove into the next season

00:14:18.060 --> 00:14:19.200
or the next area.

00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:20.620
That would be impressive, though.

00:14:21.060 --> 00:14:24.360
It would be impressive, but I actually think it wouldn't work as well

00:14:24.360 --> 00:14:27.720
as, like, as the trick that it requires, you know?

00:14:27.720 --> 00:14:30.300
Like, instead you'd be like, hold on.

00:14:30.600 --> 00:14:31.460
Did I get anywhere?

00:14:31.620 --> 00:14:33.800
Like, did I actually progress, you know?

00:14:34.120 --> 00:14:35.720
So I think that's the problem there.

00:14:35.880 --> 00:14:37.660
We should talk about the jumping, though,

00:14:37.820 --> 00:14:40.040
because that's the big aspect of this game

00:14:40.040 --> 00:14:42.940
is that your car has the ability to basically be, like,

00:14:42.960 --> 00:14:48.400
a giant pogo stick and jump very high into the air.

00:14:48.660 --> 00:14:50.080
Well, that and the bumping.

00:14:50.660 --> 00:14:52.380
Right. Well, the bumping we'll get to.

00:14:52.800 --> 00:14:54.400
Oh, okay. Yeah, don't worry.

00:14:54.540 --> 00:14:56.080
That's, you know, half the equation.

00:14:56.080 --> 00:14:57.920
But the jumping's important here

00:14:57.920 --> 00:14:59.400
because for some reason,

00:14:59.620 --> 00:15:02.360
Robert Moses, when he was designing these roads,

00:15:02.600 --> 00:15:05.480
forgot to, like, include half of the road.

00:15:05.700 --> 00:15:10.940
That's a great civil engineering inside joke right there.

00:15:11.160 --> 00:15:13.440
Yeah. Yeah, there you go.

00:15:13.800 --> 00:15:17.740
He, like, there's just these gigantic holes in the highways,

00:15:17.740 --> 00:15:22.380
and that's, like, that's the idea, you know,

00:15:22.460 --> 00:15:24.320
of, like, why you must always jump

00:15:24.320 --> 00:15:27.920
because the road ends but then restarts.

00:15:28.120 --> 00:15:29.320
Or there's an enormous wall.

00:15:29.880 --> 00:15:33.660
Yeah, an enormous wall, or it splits a certain way,

00:15:33.680 --> 00:15:36.640
or there's, like, a mini island that you have to hop to

00:15:36.640 --> 00:15:39.560
and then hop back to the main highway.

00:15:39.680 --> 00:15:41.460
Which is way too hard to fucking figure out.

00:15:41.740 --> 00:15:42.960
So, I'm sorry.

00:15:44.340 --> 00:15:49.980
Yes. It's all sort of, like, MC, not MC Escher.

00:15:50.280 --> 00:15:53.920
It kind of is in that there's just, like, walls bisecting roads

00:15:53.920 --> 00:15:57.560
and maybe a little bit of, like, Kafka

00:15:57.560 --> 00:16:02.280
because, like, what kind of society would design a city like this?

00:16:03.020 --> 00:16:06.620
It's a little bit of existential dread while I'm playing this,

00:16:06.640 --> 00:16:09.320
but I don't know if you guys felt the same way.

00:16:09.920 --> 00:16:14.640
Well, I'm just wondering, because of the bird's eye view that you get,

00:16:14.900 --> 00:16:16.400
you know, that top-down look,

00:16:16.560 --> 00:16:20.580
you can't see these things very far in advance.

00:16:20.760 --> 00:16:23.500
And you do get used to it, so I'll give it that credit.

00:16:23.860 --> 00:16:29.500
But, like, is this the best way to go about making an obstacle in a video game

00:16:29.500 --> 00:16:35.080
of just, like, reaction times for your last second notices of, like,

00:16:35.740 --> 00:16:37.180
obstacles coming at you?

00:16:37.180 --> 00:16:39.560
Like, it almost feels, if you didn't know any better,

00:16:39.800 --> 00:16:41.660
and this is, sorry, this is going out there,

00:16:41.740 --> 00:16:44.100
but it's kind of like Guitar Hero, this game, you know?

00:16:44.260 --> 00:16:46.960
You don't see the notes for a while,

00:16:46.960 --> 00:16:48.380
and then all of a sudden they're just there.

00:16:48.500 --> 00:16:50.940
And it's fine when you're playing on, like, easy mode,

00:16:50.960 --> 00:16:53.060
but if you're playing on expert, all of a sudden,

00:16:53.500 --> 00:16:56.640
things are being X'd of you that you didn't even know your fingers could do.

00:16:57.280 --> 00:16:59.480
Not that the game ever gets that intense,

00:16:59.980 --> 00:17:02.700
where there's, like, a difference between an easy and an expert mode,

00:17:03.280 --> 00:17:04.560
but it is like that,

00:17:04.560 --> 00:17:07.780
where you only have a small window of opportunity to decide

00:17:07.780 --> 00:17:10.140
when and how you want to jump,

00:17:10.140 --> 00:17:14.140
because the jump isn't something that you can actually control.

00:17:14.320 --> 00:17:18.320
You can't do, like, a hard press for a very high jump

00:17:18.320 --> 00:17:19.840
that keeps you in the air for a while.

00:17:19.940 --> 00:17:20.960
I mean, I would say...

00:17:20.960 --> 00:17:26.020
I would say that it's even worse than the Guitar Hero statement,

00:17:26.020 --> 00:17:28.580
because there are situations in this

00:17:28.580 --> 00:17:31.800
where you need the information that you can't see yet

00:17:31.800 --> 00:17:33.020
in order to jump in time.

00:17:33.320 --> 00:17:36.380
Like, that when you need to jump onto those little islands,

00:17:37.060 --> 00:17:40.280
you need to know that before you see it.

00:17:40.580 --> 00:17:44.280
Like, the audio warning that starts playing

00:17:44.280 --> 00:17:46.040
when it's trying to tell you, like,

00:17:46.040 --> 00:17:47.060
you're going to have to jump soon.

00:17:48.440 --> 00:17:51.620
It's not specific enough to say that, like,

00:17:51.720 --> 00:17:54.780
oh, you're going to have to jump to this little archipelago first.

00:17:55.800 --> 00:17:58.220
So you can only know that that's coming

00:17:58.220 --> 00:18:00.000
because you've already failed at it.

00:18:00.320 --> 00:18:03.900
So I think that that's kind of, like, a flaw in itself.

00:18:04.140 --> 00:18:05.060
There were some...

00:18:05.060 --> 00:18:07.420
Going off of that, there were some times also where

00:18:07.420 --> 00:18:11.040
you have to jump at the very last bit

00:18:11.040 --> 00:18:12.980
right before the water starts

00:18:12.980 --> 00:18:13.840
in order to clear it

00:18:13.840 --> 00:18:15.320
in some of the later levels.

00:18:16.040 --> 00:18:17.020
And for me,

00:18:17.200 --> 00:18:18.740
I felt like there was no reason

00:18:18.740 --> 00:18:20.800
to not always be jumping

00:18:20.800 --> 00:18:22.980
except during those, like,

00:18:23.220 --> 00:18:24.540
about-to-jump parts

00:18:24.540 --> 00:18:25.880
where it gives you the warning.

00:18:26.260 --> 00:18:27.280
Like, land when you see that.

00:18:27.440 --> 00:18:28.380
But besides that,

00:18:28.540 --> 00:18:29.700
just always be jumping

00:18:29.700 --> 00:18:32.140
because you just land on everyone

00:18:32.140 --> 00:18:33.660
instead of getting bumped.

00:18:34.560 --> 00:18:36.500
So... And I also...

00:18:36.500 --> 00:18:37.580
Also on jumping,

00:18:37.620 --> 00:18:38.420
this is my second thought,

00:18:38.480 --> 00:18:39.760
I thought that...

00:18:40.300 --> 00:18:41.740
I didn't like how you had to be going

00:18:41.740 --> 00:18:43.320
a certain speed in order to jump

00:18:43.320 --> 00:18:45.120
because if you're slowing down

00:18:45.120 --> 00:18:46.900
because you're trying to maneuver around someone

00:18:46.900 --> 00:18:48.340
or, you know, play the game,

00:18:49.660 --> 00:18:51.100
then... Conserve fuel.

00:18:51.260 --> 00:18:52.580
Right, conserve fuel. Because that's another thing.

00:18:52.680 --> 00:18:54.980
You're trying... Or I think there was something in the manual

00:18:54.980 --> 00:18:56.780
about having a cop pull you over

00:18:56.780 --> 00:18:57.700
which never happened.

00:18:57.800 --> 00:18:58.880
Yeah, it never happened to me.

00:18:59.320 --> 00:19:00.500
I never saw an ambulance either.

00:19:00.940 --> 00:19:02.640
I saw an ambulance. Maybe tongue-in-cheek,

00:19:02.820 --> 00:19:04.300
like, oh, don't go too fast

00:19:04.300 --> 00:19:06.040
or the cop will pull you over.

00:19:06.240 --> 00:19:07.260
Yeah, I don't know.

00:19:08.160 --> 00:19:09.420
But yeah, then...

00:19:09.420 --> 00:19:11.320
Then one of the warning signs comes up

00:19:11.320 --> 00:19:12.680
and you can't speed up fast enough

00:19:12.680 --> 00:19:13.520
to jump again.

00:19:14.080 --> 00:19:16.320
So you just kind of have to play the game

00:19:16.320 --> 00:19:17.260
a specific way.

00:19:17.800 --> 00:19:18.840
And for me,

00:19:18.920 --> 00:19:19.780
I found the easiest way

00:19:19.780 --> 00:19:21.520
was just to just always be jumping

00:19:21.520 --> 00:19:48.940
on top of people. Yeah, I'd like to go back to your point there

00:19:48.940 --> 00:19:50.380
about jumping always

00:19:50.380 --> 00:19:51.580
so this way you just land.

00:19:51.760 --> 00:19:52.760
Like, the worst thing that could happen

00:19:53.160 --> 00:19:54.060
provided that you know

00:19:54.060 --> 00:19:56.880
that there isn't a obstacle coming ahead.

00:19:57.100 --> 00:19:58.100
The worst thing that could happen

00:19:58.100 --> 00:19:59.400
is you just land on another car

00:19:59.400 --> 00:20:00.700
or you dodge cars.

00:20:01.000 --> 00:20:02.760
So when you land on the other cars,

00:20:03.060 --> 00:20:05.100
that one, like, really does a lot of damage

00:20:05.100 --> 00:20:06.580
and, like, pushes them over to the side

00:20:06.580 --> 00:20:08.000
and usually destroys the car.

00:20:08.360 --> 00:20:10.220
I find that to be a lot more effective

00:20:10.220 --> 00:20:11.740
than this bumping thing.

00:20:11.740 --> 00:20:13.100
So in some ways,

00:20:13.200 --> 00:20:14.660
the game is jump and jump

00:20:14.660 --> 00:20:16.420
and not bump and jump.

00:20:16.640 --> 00:20:18.100
But don't you use more power

00:20:18.100 --> 00:20:20.320
when you jump? Like, in your kind of...

00:20:20.320 --> 00:20:21.640
When you're in air,

00:20:21.980 --> 00:20:23.160
90% of the time,

00:20:23.360 --> 00:20:24.420
you're kind of missing

00:20:24.420 --> 00:20:25.740
all these hiccups

00:20:25.740 --> 00:20:27.480
when they are, like, pick up a bowl.

00:20:27.860 --> 00:20:28.820
And you need them.

00:20:28.920 --> 00:20:30.580
Yeah, sure. So, I mean, I feel like

00:20:30.580 --> 00:20:32.640
that would be a quick way

00:20:32.640 --> 00:20:35.400
to lose, like, power

00:20:35.400 --> 00:20:37.040
to jump at some point.

00:20:37.200 --> 00:20:39.540
I guess so, but I never had a problem with it.

00:20:40.060 --> 00:20:40.920
Oh, okay.

00:20:41.600 --> 00:20:43.320
I see what you're saying, though, Sean.

00:20:43.520 --> 00:20:44.420
It's like the jump

00:20:44.420 --> 00:20:45.960
is the greater power,

00:20:46.140 --> 00:20:47.880
but it comes with responsibility.

00:20:48.800 --> 00:20:50.080
Yes. It's a great power.

00:20:50.800 --> 00:20:53.060
Yeah. It's like the boost in F-Zero.

00:20:53.480 --> 00:20:54.460
It's also your help.

00:20:55.120 --> 00:20:57.060
Sure. Yeah, no, that's a good point, too.

00:20:57.220 --> 00:20:58.100
I just think there's something

00:20:58.100 --> 00:20:59.480
about the bump here

00:20:59.480 --> 00:21:00.620
that I hated.

00:21:01.160 --> 00:21:02.440
It didn't respond

00:21:02.440 --> 00:21:03.900
that great for me.

00:21:04.100 --> 00:21:06.380
I didn't actually... I felt like I was being bullied

00:21:06.380 --> 00:21:07.680
by every car on the road.

00:21:07.680 --> 00:21:09.020
Yeah, when I get bumped,

00:21:09.020 --> 00:21:10.960
I go flying, but when I bump someone else,

00:21:11.040 --> 00:21:12.960
they're like, oh, you did not just do that.

00:21:13.080 --> 00:21:14.660
You know, they just don't really care.

00:21:15.400 --> 00:21:16.500
Yeah, they're like that meme

00:21:16.500 --> 00:21:17.740
where the guy is, like, driving

00:21:17.740 --> 00:21:18.960
and then he hears somebody

00:21:18.960 --> 00:21:20.760
say something, so he goes in reverse.

00:21:21.160 --> 00:21:22.000
You know, it's like,

00:21:22.080 --> 00:21:22.960
that's the bump.

00:21:23.100 --> 00:21:25.260
It's like by bumping other...

00:21:25.260 --> 00:21:26.420
I don't know about that meme. I'm bumping the enemies.

00:21:26.880 --> 00:21:27.800
You come on!

00:21:28.140 --> 00:21:29.140
Is he going in reverse

00:21:29.140 --> 00:21:30.100
or is he just checking out

00:21:30.100 --> 00:21:31.140
what's happening in the back seat?

00:21:31.600 --> 00:21:32.880
Yeah, no, he goes like this.

00:21:32.960 --> 00:21:33.880
He has, like, his, you know,

00:21:33.940 --> 00:21:34.900
he has his arm around

00:21:34.900 --> 00:21:36.060
the passenger seat.

00:21:36.100 --> 00:21:37.100
He looks behind him

00:21:37.100 --> 00:21:38.480
and he's like...

00:21:38.480 --> 00:21:39.860
He goes in reverse.

00:21:41.540 --> 00:21:42.720
He's going in reverse, though?

00:21:43.120 --> 00:21:44.160
He's going in reverse.

00:21:44.460 --> 00:21:45.780
He's like, he's going forward.

00:21:45.780 --> 00:21:46.640
He sees it.

00:21:46.720 --> 00:21:48.240
He's like, he's got his eyes squinted

00:21:48.240 --> 00:21:49.480
and he's like...

00:21:49.480 --> 00:21:50.660
And he goes in reverse.

00:21:50.840 --> 00:21:52.560
He's like, I'm not going to whatever that is.

00:21:52.740 --> 00:21:53.700
Oh, no. I don't...

00:21:53.700 --> 00:21:55.160
I think I'm thinking of a different meme.

00:21:55.660 --> 00:21:56.520
I think it's really hard

00:21:56.520 --> 00:21:57.980
to describe memes over a podcast.

00:21:58.080 --> 00:21:59.360
That's a great idea for a podcast

00:21:59.360 --> 00:22:00.400
that somebody else can handle.

00:22:00.400 --> 00:22:01.400
We sort of did this...

00:22:01.400 --> 00:22:02.860
I'm not doing it. I mean, this is off topic,

00:22:02.920 --> 00:22:04.280
but we did play that game

00:22:04.280 --> 00:22:06.560
before the in-person records

00:22:06.560 --> 00:22:08.680
in the before time. I'm just saying,

00:22:08.820 --> 00:22:09.900
free idea for a podcast.

00:22:10.160 --> 00:22:10.980
Yeah. Describe memes to people.

00:22:10.980 --> 00:22:12.080
No, no, no. I copyright that.

00:22:12.460 --> 00:22:14.660
Oh, yeah. You copyright a lot of podcasts.

00:22:14.800 --> 00:22:16.100
You have that one just about all different

00:22:16.100 --> 00:22:17.700
kinds of mustards? I do.

00:22:18.300 --> 00:22:19.260
Nobody can make it. Yeah, we were going to taste

00:22:19.260 --> 00:22:20.900
every mustard in North America.

00:22:21.040 --> 00:22:23.460
I'm a podcast idea squatter.

00:22:23.780 --> 00:22:26.160
Very good. That's the Thomas Edison way.

00:22:27.180 --> 00:22:28.820
All right. Thomas Edison,

00:22:29.000 --> 00:22:30.260
Robert Moses, what year is it?

00:22:30.640 --> 00:22:31.580
You know, funny thing about

00:22:31.580 --> 00:22:32.560
the Robert Moses thing

00:22:32.560 --> 00:22:33.640
since you mentioned about

00:22:33.640 --> 00:22:36.580
like how the devastation

00:22:36.580 --> 00:22:38.240
that the city must be dealing with

00:22:38.240 --> 00:22:39.160
for designing

00:22:39.780 --> 00:22:41.140
their highways like this.

00:22:41.280 --> 00:22:43.200
It made me think of City Connection,

00:22:43.400 --> 00:22:45.280
that Jaleco game we played

00:22:45.280 --> 00:22:46.180
where the girl's on

00:22:46.180 --> 00:22:49.240
like her moped. And that one has like...

00:22:49.240 --> 00:22:51.880
Oh, yeah. That's a disaster itself

00:22:51.880 --> 00:22:52.940
as well. Yeah, it's like,

00:22:53.060 --> 00:22:53.920
welcome to New York.

00:22:54.060 --> 00:22:55.560
And it's like, the roads are on different

00:22:56.500 --> 00:22:57.340
floating platforms.

00:22:57.740 --> 00:22:59.100
Yeah. And it's like, that's not New York

00:22:59.100 --> 00:23:00.720
to me, baby. Well, the other thing,

00:23:00.920 --> 00:23:03.220
like in the same vein

00:23:03.220 --> 00:23:04.680
that I noticed was

00:23:05.280 --> 00:23:06.200
in the manual,

00:23:06.200 --> 00:23:07.700
the story is like,

00:23:08.040 --> 00:23:09.440
yeah, you're on a Sunday drive

00:23:09.440 --> 00:23:11.260
and this gang

00:23:11.260 --> 00:23:12.340
steals your girlfriend

00:23:12.340 --> 00:23:14.060
and then you just decide

00:23:14.060 --> 00:23:15.380
to, I guess,

00:23:15.580 --> 00:23:16.580
follow the car

00:23:17.480 --> 00:23:18.720
and then put her...

00:23:18.720 --> 00:23:20.260
But before you do that, you name your car.

00:23:20.380 --> 00:23:21.260
Yeah, you for some reason

00:23:21.260 --> 00:23:24.220
named your car. It's never decided why.

00:23:24.700 --> 00:23:26.900
You never see the car's name

00:23:26.900 --> 00:23:27.720
in the game.

00:23:29.220 --> 00:23:33.000
And so you try and like find her

00:23:33.000 --> 00:23:34.020
and you just, I guess,

00:23:34.080 --> 00:23:35.700
put her in harm's way

00:23:35.700 --> 00:23:37.860
by trying to run the car

00:23:37.860 --> 00:23:39.280
off the road that she's in.

00:23:39.440 --> 00:23:40.840
And then apparently

00:23:41.420 --> 00:23:42.480
you're supposed to run

00:23:42.480 --> 00:23:43.900
all the cars off the road

00:23:43.900 --> 00:23:45.860
and they all want to do that to you.

00:23:46.060 --> 00:23:47.480
Like, it all sounds like a nightmare.

00:23:47.640 --> 00:23:50.220
Like the architecture

00:23:50.220 --> 00:23:51.180
in this game,

00:23:51.420 --> 00:23:52.960
the civil engineering

00:23:52.960 --> 00:23:53.820
in this game,

00:23:54.640 --> 00:23:55.520
the society

00:23:55.520 --> 00:23:56.540
in which you live

00:23:56.540 --> 00:23:57.560
in which it's okay

00:23:57.560 --> 00:23:59.400
to act like you have...

00:23:59.400 --> 00:24:00.780
Everybody has

00:24:01.700 --> 00:24:02.520
road rage.

00:24:02.820 --> 00:24:03.660
It's just a world

00:24:03.660 --> 00:24:04.520
I don't want to live in.

00:24:04.660 --> 00:24:05.740
I don't know if it's a...

00:24:07.000 --> 00:24:08.560
An analogy for anything

00:24:08.560 --> 00:24:09.500
or an allegory.

00:24:09.600 --> 00:24:10.760
All these one-way streets also.

00:24:11.400 --> 00:24:13.080
Yeah. I mean, it's a never-ending...

00:24:13.080 --> 00:24:14.860
I mean, everything's on the same road.

00:24:15.440 --> 00:24:16.820
Weird. And you know what?

00:24:16.820 --> 00:24:17.980
If you get far enough

00:24:17.980 --> 00:24:19.480
into the stages

00:24:19.480 --> 00:24:21.060
in the game, you can see

00:24:21.520 --> 00:24:23.200
the car that is carrying

00:24:23.200 --> 00:24:24.800
the girlfriend's...

00:24:24.800 --> 00:24:25.860
Well, I thought she kept getting stolen.

00:24:26.220 --> 00:24:27.080
That makes more sense.

00:24:27.420 --> 00:24:29.060
That also makes more sense,

00:24:29.120 --> 00:24:30.160
I suppose. But,

00:24:30.160 --> 00:24:31.980
I mean, that makes...

00:24:31.980 --> 00:24:32.940
That convinces me

00:24:32.940 --> 00:24:33.780
that this game

00:24:33.780 --> 00:24:34.740
is actually one of those

00:24:34.740 --> 00:24:36.860
nightmares where you're not fast enough.

00:24:37.080 --> 00:24:38.200
Uh-huh. You know, it's like,

00:24:38.280 --> 00:24:39.680
well, you know, your car is always

00:24:39.680 --> 00:24:42.000
going to be slower than the other car

00:24:42.500 --> 00:24:44.120
owned by the jackal gang

00:24:44.120 --> 00:24:46.000
that has your girlfriend

00:24:46.480 --> 00:24:48.520
kidnapped. I buy that.

00:24:48.920 --> 00:24:49.840
The story's also

00:24:49.840 --> 00:24:50.660
very out of place

00:24:50.660 --> 00:24:51.500
if we're being honest. Like,

00:24:51.620 --> 00:24:55.100
I didn't need one. I didn't notice it.

00:24:55.260 --> 00:24:56.600
I don't know if you picked up

00:24:56.600 --> 00:24:57.620
on any of those clues

00:24:58.060 --> 00:24:59.300
before reading the manual.

00:24:59.480 --> 00:25:00.440
Like, visually in the game,

00:25:00.540 --> 00:25:02.340
did you ever say, like, those must be the jackals

00:25:02.340 --> 00:25:03.340
and my car must be

00:25:03.340 --> 00:25:05.300
named the popper? Well,

00:25:05.360 --> 00:25:06.840
that does go without saying,

00:25:06.980 --> 00:25:08.540
like, once you get into it

00:25:08.540 --> 00:25:09.640
that it is named the popper.

00:25:09.800 --> 00:25:10.880
You're right. I was wrong before,

00:25:10.980 --> 00:25:12.080
but the rest,

00:25:12.200 --> 00:25:13.620
no. So, it's just one of those

00:25:13.620 --> 00:25:14.520
things where, like,

00:25:14.520 --> 00:25:16.540
I appreciate that they included

00:25:16.540 --> 00:25:17.540
a story in the manual,

00:25:18.380 --> 00:25:19.380
but they went,

00:25:19.380 --> 00:25:20.700
they went a little extra

00:25:20.700 --> 00:25:21.540
to make sure that

00:25:21.540 --> 00:25:23.100
it matched the visuals

00:25:23.100 --> 00:25:24.020
in the game.

00:25:24.280 --> 00:25:25.300
Yeah. And they should have

00:25:25.300 --> 00:25:26.520
just had fun with it

00:25:26.520 --> 00:25:27.520
instead. Mm-hmm.

00:25:28.220 --> 00:25:29.780
It should have been, like, a whole,

00:25:29.900 --> 00:25:30.880
it should have, honestly, this should have been

00:25:30.880 --> 00:25:32.360
a game that took place in World War II

00:25:32.920 --> 00:25:34.580
where, you know,

00:25:35.600 --> 00:25:36.680
people from the future

00:25:37.160 --> 00:25:38.380
came and kidnapped

00:25:38.380 --> 00:25:41.380
your girlfriend, and she is the only one

00:25:41.380 --> 00:25:43.620
who has the codes

00:25:44.140 --> 00:25:46.640
to stop the moon

